# CPU Scheduling
method of selecting the order of processes in the ready queue
aims to use processor efficiently and minimize waiting & response time

Arrival Time (AT)
- process arrival in the ready queue
Completion Time (Ct)
- time of completion with execution of process
Burst Time (BT)
- time needed by process for CPU execution
- Cumulative Burst time 
Turn Around Time (TAT)
- time difference between completion time and arrival time
- `TAT = CT - AT`
Waiting time (WT)
 - Idle time
 - `WT = BT - AT`
 - `WT = TAT - BT`
Response time
 - the time where the task was first processed
 - the time where the system started the task
---
**Non-Preemptive Scheduling**
- Need to finish a task before it could proceed to another task
- **Examples**:
	- First come first serve
	- shortest job first (shortest burst time)
	- Longest job first (longest burst time)
	- Highest response ratio next 

Preemptive
- the current processing can be halted because of a task with higher priority
- needs to have a result calendar
- **Examples**:
	- Priority Scheduling
	- Shortest remaining job first (process with lest time left to finish is selected to run; running process continue until process with shorter remaining time arrives)
	- Longest remaining job first
	- Round robin (**quantum time** - time allocated for a specific job. ex: 1 sec only allocated for a specific job)

| Algo                    | Allocation                                                                      | Complexity                                         | AWT                                               | Preemption | Starvation | Performance                                       |
| ----------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- | ---------- | ---------- | ------------------------------------------------- |
| FCFS                    | According to the arrival time of the processes, the CPU is allocated.           | Simple and easy to implement                       | Large.                                            | No         | No         | Slow                                              |
| SJF                     | Based on the lowest CPU burst time  (BT).                                       | More complex than FCFS                             | Smaller than FCFS                                 | No         | Yes        | Minimum Average Waiting Time                      |
| RR                      | According to the order of the process arrives with fixed time quantum (TQ)      | The complexity depends on Time Quantum size        | Large as compared to SJF and Priority scheduling. | Yes        | No         | Each process has given a fairly fixed time        |
| Priority Pre-emptive    | According to the priority. The bigger priority task executes first              | This type is less complex                          | Smaller than FCFS                                 | Yes        | Yes        | Well performance but contain a starvation problem |
| Priority non-preemptive | According to the priority with monitoring the new incoming higher priority jobs | This type is less complex than Priority preemptive | Preemptive Smaller than FCFS                      | No         | Yes        | Most beneficial with batch systems                |
